GitLab is a web-based DevOps platform that combines Git repository hosting with the rest of the software delivery lifecycle in a single application. 
Instead of stitching together separate tools for code hosting, CI/CD, issue tracking, security scanning, and package storage, 
GitLab provides all of them under one roof.

Core capabilities:

Source control: Git repositories with merge requests (GitLab’s equivalent of pull requests), code review, branching, and protected branches.
CI/CD: Built-in pipelines defined in a .gitlab-ci.yml file in your repo. Jobs run on GitLab Runners, which can be SaaS-hosted or self-hosted.
Project management: Issues, boards, milestones, epics, and roadmaps.
Registries: Container registry, package registry, and Terraform module registry.
Security: SAST, DAST, dependency scanning, secret detection, and container scanning (some features are in paid tiers).
Deployment: Environments, review apps, and integrations with Kubernetes and cloud providers.

Hosting options:

GitLab.com: the SaaS version, managed by GitLab.
Self-managed: you install and run GitLab on your own servers or cloud (Community Edition is free and open source; 
Enterprise Edition adds paid features).


=============	Where GitLab stands out

Single application: CI/CD, container registry, package registry, security scanning, and issue boards share one UI, one permission model, and one data store. With GitHub or Bitbucket, you more often assemble pieces from add-ons or third-party tools.
Pipeline model: GitLab pipelines have stages, with strong support for includes, templates, parent/child pipelines, and environments with deployment tracking.
Self-managed option: Many organizations choose GitLab because they can run the free edition on their own infrastructure.
Group hierarchy: Subgroups let you set permissions and CI/CD variables at any level of the tree, which suits large organizations.


================= What is GitLab Runner?

GitLab Runner is an application that works with GitLab CI/CD to run jobs in a pipeline.

When developers push code to GitLab, they can define automated tasks in a .gitlab-ci.yml file. These tasks might include running tests, building applications, or deploying code. 
GitLab Runner is the application that executes these tasks on computing infrastructure.
note: Runners are processes that pick up and execute CI/CD jobs for GitLab.

